      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "There is no single definitive answer, as it depends entirely on how one interprets the fidelity of the social contract. If the contract prioritizes individual liberty above all else, then expansive surveillance is an inherent violation because it transforms citizens into subjects under constant monitoring. Conversely, if the contract grants the state a legitimate authority to protect collective life, then targeted surveillance can be seen as a necessary adaptation to complex modern threats.",
